proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Two action rules need correction. First, during initial open-hand pickup approaches, with memory initialized to placements_completed=0 and milestone=start, the policy localized the instructed red or green cube too far in +x, producing errors slightly greater than 1 cm. This breaks SUFFICIENCY through an incorrect geometric localization rule, not missing history. Replace the prescribed resting-cube top-face plane of z=0.04 m with approximately z=0.02 m for these cubes. Identify the center of the actual top face, excluding visible side faces, and intersect its calibrated camera ray with that plane. Cross-check front and wrist estimates where both show the face clearly; do not average projections of different physical features. Retain the separate commanded grasp TCP height of z=0.02 m: feature-localization height and commanded TCP height have distinct roles. Apply this corrected localization to both initial pickup and regrasping a resting cube on the target. Continue deriving grasp yaw from calibrated cube edges rather than defaulting to zero.\n\nSecond, after a repeated pickup has just been lifted to transport height, the observed milestone is cube_lifted even when the TCP already lies within 2 mm of the target-hover position. The existing omission rule sent the policy directly down with an open-gripper release, whereas the recorded behavior requires a separate closed-gripper alignment before release. This breaks SUFFICIENCY. Delete the rule that omits target-hover alignment below 2 mm, including the later sentence preserving that exception. At cube_lifted, always choose a separate target-centered hover with fingers closed and nominal z=0.15 m. Construct the center-corrected waypoint first. If its displacement from the current TCP is less than 2 mm, keep its target-center x,y and raise its z by 3–5 mm, enough to satisfy the interface minimum. This remains within 1 cm of the recorded hover while avoiding an invalid near-identical waypoint. Only after observing completion of this alignment should target_hover be recognized and the descent-and-release rule applied. Ordinary small tracking errors and a very short nonzero alignment motion must not prevent recognition of completion.",
  "refused": "The initial-pickup report also requested using the top-face center and deriving yaw from cube edges. Those requirements already exist, so no new memory or duplicate rule is needed; the substantive change is correcting the top-face height and making feature selection explicit. Another initial green-cube approach exceeded the positional tolerance without identifying a missing fact; it is covered by the same geometric correction rather than an episode-specific coordinate. The repeated-placement report proposed allowing a sub-2-mm target-hover waypoint as an exception to the interface safeguard. That exception is not passed on because the interface requires at least 2 mm of movement. Preserve the separate closed-gripper alignment using the existing small upward-offset safeguard instead. No reported failure requires changing memory values, transition timing, or comparison types."
}

